# Compatibility register and native fallback

Every compatibility claim needs a scope, a version context, a kind of evidence and a fallback that works. Theme variables, exact adapters, custom components and documentation examples are different kinds of support. Matching colours do not show that a native task is supported.

These examples are simulated with invented records. They do not install an adapter or call Home Assistant. This page covers the design contract for gap HA-GAP-030.

## Scope and native ownership

Home Assistant supports theme variables and extra frontend modules. Custom cards use a separate API. Private native structures are not public compatibility promises. The register below lists the exact targets in this repository and separates inspected source from executed evidence. Source: [Home Assistant frontend integration](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/frontend/), reviewed on 11 November 2026.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Token-only coverage | Which public variables the component uses. | Promise access to a closed or private root. |
| Exact adapter supported | The host, what the adapter changes, the evidence and the clean-up. | Extend an exact selector to similar cards. |
| Target changed or absent | The native interface, with a fallback for that surface. | Delete controls or throw an error into the host’s lifecycle. |
| Default theme or recovery mode | Remove what the adapter owns and keep the current native values. | Restore stale user data or hide native diagnostics. |
| Reference example | A label saying it is simulated, and what an integration needs. | Present a working local form as a connected Home Assistant component. |

## Integration contract

1. For every new adapter, record the repository version or commit, the Core, frontend and custom-card versions, the exact host and engine, the discovery boundary, the styles and attributes it owns, and how it is restored.
2. Keep source review, automated fixtures, manual browser checks and live installation evidence apart. An inspected development commit is not the installed frontend.
3. Use bounded discovery and do not patch browser prototypes. Repeated imports, node replacement, navigation and theme changes must not pile up observers, listeners or duplicate controls.
4. On removal, restore only values the adapter still owns. Keep later native updates and the latest source data. Never write `.storage` or dashboard configuration to repair a presentation fault.

## Accessible interaction

- The fallback must keep accessible native controls, names, focus and diagnostics. A fixture pass on a source shape does not cover a newly changed private structure.
- Check owned elements in light, dark and forced colours, then check the surrounding native component.
- Say plainly which interactions are unsupported and which roots are closed. Do not make a native component look available while hiding that it was rejected or lacks the capability.

## Failure and recovery

- If Profile insertion is unavailable, keep the native theme dropdown and the standalone settings card.
- If an Energy renderer is absent, disposed or unsupported, remove the owned plot and table presentation and keep the native route. Do not make up readings.
- If a custom card changes structure, remove that exact compatibility sheet. Do not widen selectors to unrelated descendants.

## Current implementation register

| Surface and exact boundary | Coverage and ownership | Evidence and fallback |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Native theme variables | Generated Civica themes; component consumes supported tokens | Palette/unit checks; unsupported private variables retain native behaviour |
| `ha-pick-theme-row` | Companion adds palette/tone controls; native theme event/persistence remain authoritative | Theme-settings fixture; native dropdown and standalone `custom:civica-theme-settings` fallback |
| `ha-sidebar` | Supplied identity, icon contrast and numeric MOJ-derived attention badge | Theme/badge fixtures; restore owned attributes and disconnect observer when inactive |
| `hass-subpage`, `ha-top-app-bar-fixed` | Scoped toolbar icon/focus contrast | Nested native-shaped fixture; no replacement of toolbar actions |
| `pa-appliance-card`, `home-next-collection-card`, `pa-gas-safety-card` | Exact adopted presentation sheets only | Legacy-card fixture; card data, permissions, actions and native markup remain authoritative |
| `ha-panel-energy` and seven supported Cartesian hosts | Existing ECharts presentation, stable colours, signed Power view and read-only values table | Real-renderer/source-shape fixtures; restore owned options and retain native data when unsupported |
| `custom:civica-action-links` | Maintained real local anchors and existing fragment destinations | Action-link fixture; no device commands or invented destinations |
| `custom:civica-house-lighting-card` | Maintained light summary/control card and bounded native more-info scene presentation | Lighting fixture; supported service capabilities, reserved ownership and native dialog lifecycle remain explicit |
| History, native Energy distribution, Map, authentication, arbitrary third-party cards | No Energy/private-brand adapter claim for these families | Native behaviour and supported public tokens; separate task-specific live acceptance |

The exact Energy hosts are `hui-power-sources-graph-card`, `hui-energy-usage-graph-card`, `hui-energy-solar-graph-card`, `hui-energy-gas-graph-card`, `hui-energy-water-graph-card`, `hui-energy-devices-graph-card` and `hui-energy-devices-detail-graph-card`. The native Energy distribution diagram is not an ONS Sankey. History and non-ECharts/custom renderers do not gain the Energy values table through this adapter.

Read [Compatibility and limits](../get-started/compatibility.html), [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html), [Cards](../components/cards.html) and [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html) with this register. Implementation evidence is in [the companion](https://github.com/nathcymru/civica/blob/main/ui/src/civica-ui.ts), the [Energy adapter](https://github.com/nathcymru/civica/blob/main/ui/src/energy-charts.ts) and the [lighting card](https://github.com/nathcymru/civica/tree/main/card/src). These links follow the current branch, so record an immutable commit with each executed test.

## Versioned evidence boundary

The Energy source contract explicitly identifies frontend **20261028.5**. The [published fc393e4 verification record](https://github.com/nathcymru/HA-Theme-gov.uk/blob/fc393e49205926b753d1c531358c5e627c9c2af6/VERIFICATION.md) distinguishes historical fixture checks from release-specific live observations. The [published 531-check browser run](https://github.com/nathcymru/HA-Theme-gov.uk/actions/runs/43103696048) covers that documentation/fixture revision; it does not certify this expanded specification or an uninspected installation. Current official documentation was reviewed on 11 November 2026; that source review is a separate evidence type.

A compatibility release record must name the Core, frontend, browser and every relevant custom-card version. It must also give the tasks and states tested, the theme context, the result, the artefacts, the limits left open and the rollback preconditions. Until those fields are complete, keep a new installation or a changed private host marked **not tested**.

